+
+\paragraph{Ready-goal device qualification.}
+A ready-goal adapter exports arithmetic goals sequentially, solves immutable
+requests in parallel, then checks certificates in the same Lean state. It
+elaborates original arguments per goal and retains native fallback. A selected
+15-source qualification forms 1,050 problems: two groups of 512 small linear
+problems and four smaller nonlinear groups. It checks 1,040 certificates
+and falls back on ten. Exported problem counts are distinct from device-ready
+LPs. The fresh matched native, CPU and additive GPU conditions take 116.99,
+79.93 and 79.59 seconds, respectively, with seven complete and eight rejected
+outcomes in each. Full CPU/GPU responses agree; six message-only differences
+from native remain. All 56 races retain native and HiGHS competitors.
+Two device groups launch three models, including a two-candidate group;
+all retire. No GPU answer wins. The 0.42\% elapsed difference in one selected
+preflight therefore establishes no useful GPU benefit or full-matrix
+admission. The first stale-library integration attempt is retained separately,
+rather than included as a valid additive comparison.
+
+\paragraph{Independent device groups.}
+A request-sized CUDA virtual-memory implementation uses the existing driver allocation API~\cite{cudaVMM} to execute overlapping device
+groups under the same 48\,GiB address-space ceiling. A repaired 64-source
+qualification executes 20 nonempty groups and 36 LP models, with complete
+device-timer samples, at most four overlapping groups, and no live allocations
+at the final provider probe. Whole warm-pipeline time is 43.07 seconds,
+versus 37.78 seconds for a fresh CPU oracle and 71.64 seconds for native.
+All verdicts and non-message fields agree. The GPU condition is 14.00\%
+slower than CPU. This preflight does not admit a full GPU matrix.
+Its composition retains native hybrid but replaces the external HiGHS
+contender. It therefore does not establish the effect of adding GPU work
+while preserving the strongest measured CPU portfolio. Extra contender,
+transport, exact repair and retirement costs must be included in that comparison.
+
+A corrected additive portfolio keeps native hybrid and CPU HiGHS while adding
+the same GPU implementation. On the original 64 sources, the same private
+numerical build takes 38.02 seconds without GPU and 39.33 seconds with GPU,
+or 3.43\% more elapsed. Full CPU/GPU native responses agree. Of 68 races,
+HiGHS wins 46, native search 21 and an exact-checked GPU negative certificate
+is selected once. Six real GPU groups with nine models retire completely.
+These observations retain the stronger CPU competitors but do not establish
+a device benefit or admit a full matrix. They also show why racing cannot
+guarantee zero slowdown when host overhead and contention remain.
+
+\paragraph{Remaining numerical work.}
+The remaining GPU opportunity depends on numerical work in the current CPU path, including unsuccessful searches and retirement. The third two-sided CPU-oracle capture contains 1,888 uniquely source-matched
+numerical races. Their durations sum to 101.54 seconds, including launch,
+exact repair, cancellation and retirement. The 935 parsed external HiGHS
+searches sum to 9.25 wall seconds, while 467 additional connections are unparsed
+or aborted. These are not disjoint pipeline fractions.
+The observed critical lane, the static candidate queue finishing last, contains 14.11 seconds of complete race work.
+Deleting all recorded race work while preserving other observed durations,
+gaps and lane assignments models a 450.47-second pipeline, or
+$1.53475\times$ relative to that pair's native reference.
+This is an intentionally optimistic fixed-lane sensitivity, not a measured
+GPU result or a scheduler-independent Amdahl bound. Current product and
+small-LP regions are not exhaustively attributed by these records.
+It does not justify closing the GPU direction.
 
 Earlier arithmetic versions, complete phase captures and the supported-expression GPU control remain in Appendix~\ref{app:arithmetic-history}; the supporting CPU comparisons remain in Appendix~\ref{app:cpu-controls}. These observations constrain their own mechanisms and do not supply a current GPU impossibility bound.
 
